WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.950
All right, let's get to audience questions
for the week.

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:07.500
First one, Hey, David is a right to privacy,
still a realistic expectation in 2019 given

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.800
the technology that is now ubiquitous.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.709
So sometimes with these [inaudible]

00:00:11.709 --> 00:00:17.250
easier to start with the simplest form of
the answer, like the most basic logistical

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.760
answer and then talk about the implications.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.100
So in a literal sense, in public there's no
realistic expectation of privacy.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.620
There are cameras everywhere.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:33.910
Uh, there are all sorts of different surveillance
methods that are going on via satellite.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.880
If a government really wants to follow you
around, they can do it when you're out in

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:38.880
public.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.470
So in public there is not, in a practical
sense an expectation of privacy, but that

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.810
definition of privacy has changed over time.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.649
And I'll get to that in private people.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:54.470
Can decide whether to record and there are
laws that govern this.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:01:00.079
If businesses want to have surveillance cameras
inside their business, most of the time it's

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:02.580
legal in bathrooms and locker rooms.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.070
It's not an, it should continue to be illegal.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.890
I believe that it should be disclosed if there
is surveillance.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:12.580
I'm trying to think in Vegas casinos where
there are cameras everywhere.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.680
I don't know that I see signs that say that
you are under surveillance at all times other

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.250
than in the bathroom and presumably it presumably
in your hotel room.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.520
I think that that should be there.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:28.440
It may not even really be um, the law, but
it is that businesses right to do it and then

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.210
it's up to you to decide whether you want
to be a patron of a business that is a recording,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.120
video surveillance, et cetera.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:41.330
The problem really goes back to what do we
mean by privacy when we talk about data collection,

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:48.590
is that a violation of privacy that was not
in considered by older definitions of what

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.729
it means to have a right to privacy.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:54.290
Because what you have to understand is that
a hundred years ago we could say if you're

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.259
in public, you don't have a right to privacy.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.990
And in 2019 we say if you're in public, you
don't have a right to privacy.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.950
But what that meant a hundred years ago is
very different from what it means today.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:14.150
Today you, uh, have the ability to track someone
almost 24, seven between their phone using

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:21.180
the GPS on their phone and all sorts of public
and private cameras that exist out on the

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.870
streets of our cities, in businesses, et cetera.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.370
A hundred years ago.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.680
Even though the presumption of the right to
privacy might've been no different than it

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.260
is today from a legal perspective, in order
to track someone in the way that you can track

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.580
them down merely technologically, you needed
a team to actually track that person 24, seven

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.810
and so even if the law has not changed, and
even if we can say, even though by the way

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.700
the Patriot act and other types of surveillance
have modified the law around other types of

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.370
surveillance, including phone records and
others.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.670
But that's a little bit different than what
we're talking about here.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:01.340
Uh, the ability, the practical ability to
do this type of surveillance has changed drastically.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:08.330
Never having a right to privacy in public,
uh, ignores that today.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.450
It means something very, very different to
be walking around in public than it did a

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.780
hundred years ago.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:21.610
So should our definition of privacy evolve
even with the same data, often there's a question

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:23.640
of who gets to see it, right?

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.410
I mean in, in principle, think about something
like medical records and the privacy that

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.519
is presumed to go along with those.

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:38.930
I might be okay with those records being used
in some sort of aggregate and non-US way to

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:46.090
research illness, but that doesn't necessarily
mean that I want my insurer to have access

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:51.210
to those records or that I want those to have
my name attached to them in a way that is

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.299
not private.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:04:00.030
Uh, so clearly in public, there is no right
to privacy in the sense that many of us imagine.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:05.069
But I still believe that we should have a
reasonable right to anonymity if we want it.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:09.160
And that's where the technological surveillance
starts to become a question Mark.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.160
Realistically.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.640
The other thing we have to consider about
this is that as these new technologies have

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:19.329
been developed, public opinion has also shifted
and will likely continue to shift.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:24.879
If you ask a 20 year old today and a four
year 40 year old and a 60 year old and an

00:04:24.879 --> 00:04:29.699
80 year old, when it comes to something as
seemingly straightforward as right to privacy

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:34.599
in public, when you're just out in public,
you will have drastically different answers

00:04:34.599 --> 00:04:36.819
from what is reasonable from each of those
groups.

00:04:36.819 --> 00:04:41.759
And I can't imagine that that's going to stay
the same over time as these new technological

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:45.069
means become more of an everyday part of our
lives.

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:50.510
When I write an email, when I think about
it, I tell myself I have to be under the,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:57.760
the sort of, uh, operating under the belief
that this is not necessarily private, but

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.099
I'm not actually thinking of that.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:05.610
Every time I write an email, younger generations
may be actually thinking about it that way

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.590
or they may not because they don't care.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.169
And, uh, that is part of the shift in public
opinion around this that we've seen.

00:05:12.169 --> 00:05:13.550
Let me know what you think.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.039
Uh, when it comes to a right to privacy, what
is reasonable to expect and what is not.

